.TH HEXWALK 1 "September 2026" "HexWalk" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
hexwalk \- interactive keyword-based binary and firmware analysis tool

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hexwalk
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " file"

.SH DESCRIPTION
HexWalk is an interactive, terminal-based analysis tool for inspecting binaries,
firmware images, and raw data files for embedded strings, artifacts, and
security-relevant indicators.

It combines optimized keyword scanning with a curses-based user interface,
allowing analysts to explore findings interactively while retaining the ability
to export structured results and summaries.

HexWalk is designed for security engineers, reverse engineers, and incident
responders who need fast, repeatable insight into opaque or unknown binaries.

.SH SUPPORTED FILE TYPES
HexWalk can analyze:

.TP
.B Raw binaries
Generic binary files, memory dumps, and disk images.

.TP
.B ELF executables
Executables and shared objects.

.TP
.B Intel HEX firmware images
Automatically detected and converted into a flat binary image prior to analysis.

.TP
.B Text files
Treated as binary-safe input.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keyword " " PATTERN
Perform an ad-hoc search instead of using the predefined keyword lists.

The pattern behaves similarly to egrep:
.IP
\- Multiple keywords may be separated using the pipe character (\fB|\fP)
.IP
\- Regex expressions may be specified using the \fBregex:\fP prefix
.IP
\- Case-insensitive matching is used by default

Examples:
.IP
\fBhexwalk -k root /etc/passwd\fP
.IP
\fBhexwalk -k 'root|admin|password' firmware.bin\fP
.IP
\fBhexwalk -k 'regex:\\b(?:10|192\\.168)\\.' image.bin\fP

When this option is used, keyword files on disk are ignored.

.TP
.B \-\-summary
Generate a textual summary report and exit without starting the interactive UI.

.TP
.B \-\-export
Generate a JSON export of all findings and exit without starting the interactive UI.

.TP
.B \-\-show\-all
Display all domains, subcategories, and keywords in the category tree,
even if no hits are found.

.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help text and exit.

.SH USER INTERFACE

HexWalk runs entirely in the terminal and is divided into three panes:

.TP
.B Categories
A hierarchical tree showing domains, subcategories, and keywords.

.TP
.B Hits
Displays matching strings and contextual excerpts.

.TP
.B Details
Shows a hex and ASCII view aligned to file offsets.

A separate summary screen can be invoked from within the UI.

.SH KEY BINDINGS

.TP
.B Arrow Up / Down
Move selection within the active pane.

.TP
.B Arrow Left
Collapse the current category or subcategory.

.TP
.B Arrow Right
Expand the current category or subcategory.

.TP
.B Enter
Toggle expand/collapse in the category pane, or open the hit inspection modal.

.TP
.B Tab
Switch between panes.

.TP
.B /
Filter results interactively.

.TP
.B +
Expand the entire category tree.

.TP
.B -
Collapse the entire category tree.

.TP
.B d
Toggle the details pane.

.TP
.B e
Export current findings to JSON.

.TP
.B s
Show the summary screen.

.TP
.B h
Show help.

.TP
.B q
Quit HexWalk (with confirmation).

.SH OUTPUT FILES

.TP
.B hexwalk_<filename>_export.json
Structured JSON export of all findings.

.TP
.B hexwalk_<filename>_summary.txt
Human-readable summary report.

.SH PERFORMANCE
HexWalk uses an optimized Aho–Corasick matcher for plain keywords and a separate
regex scanning phase. Progress is displayed in a modal during scanning.

Large keyword sets and large binaries are handled efficiently.

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
.B TERM
HexWalk uses ASCII-safe UI elements by default to ensure compatibility across
terminals, SSH sessions, and PuTTY.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B /usr/local/share/hexwalk/keywords
Default keyword definitions organized by domain and subcategory.

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Successful execution.

.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments or file error.

